+# Pre-ship review — USDC Payouts dApp (Base)
+
+Scope: `packages/nextjs` (frontend + configuration) and the deploy/CI configuration that
+affects it. Reviewed at commit `14da841` on `main`.
+
+What I ran:
+
+- `yarn next:lint` → 0 errors, **100 prettier warnings**
+- `yarn tsc --noEmit` → clean
+- `yarn next build` → see Note 1 at the bottom (local build blocked by a sandbox Node artifact, not a repo defect)
+
+Findings are ordered most-severe first.
+
+---
+
+## Ship-blocking
+
+### 1. Payout feed scans Base from block 0 — ~67,000 sequential `getLogs` calls per page load
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:29-34`
+
+```ts
+const { data: payouts } = useScaffoldEventHistory({
+  contractName: "Payouts",
+  eventName: "Paid",
+  fromBlock: 0n,
+  watch: true,
+});
+```
+
+`useScaffoldEventHistory` pages through history in `blocksBatchSize` chunks of **500 blocks**
+(`packages/nextjs/hooks/scaffold-eth/useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:95`, `:180-191`), firing each
+batch sequentially. Base is past block 33.7M, so `fromBlock: 0n` means roughly **67,000
+round-trips** before the feed renders anything. On the public RPC / shared Alchemy key this
+will rate-limit or hang indefinitely, and it re-runs for every visitor.
+
+The contract entry already carries the right starting point — `deployedOnBlock: 33712450`
+(`packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts:45`) — and the hook defaults to it when
+`fromBlock` is omitted (`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:128-135`). The explicit `0n` overrides that
+default and throws the optimization away.
+
+**Fix:** delete the `fromBlock: 0n` line so the hook picks up `deployedOnBlock`. That alone cuts
+it to whatever has elapsed since deploy — still growing, so also cap the window and raise the
+batch size:
+
+```ts
+const { data: payouts } = useScaffoldEventHistory({
+  contractName: "Payouts",
+  eventName: "Paid",
+  watch: true,
+  blocksBatchSize: 10_000,
+});
+```
+
+Longer term this hook is explicitly `@deprecated` for production use
+(`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:57-60`: *"It uses getLogs which can overload RPC endpoints
+(especially on L2s)"*). For a real launch the feed should be backed by an indexer (the repo
+already documents `ponder` and `subgraph` skills in `AGENTS.md`). At minimum, ship with the
+`deployedOnBlock` default; the indexer can follow.
+
+### 2. Page uses raw wagmi `useWriteContract`, bypassing every SE-2 write safeguard
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:6, 21, 36-62`
+
+```ts
+import { useAccount, useWriteContract } from "wagmi";
+const { writeContractAsync, isPending } = useWriteContract();
+```
+
+`AGENTS.md` mandates `useScaffoldWriteContract` for contract writes, and this is not a style
+preference — `packages/nextjs/hooks/scaffold-eth/useScaffoldWriteContract.ts:93-138` supplies
+four things the page now has none of:
+
+1. **Wrong-network guard** (`:103-106`). Raw `useWriteContract` sends to *whatever chain the
+   wallet is currently on*. `enabledChains` always includes mainnet
+   (`services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx:12-14`), so a user connected to Ethereum gets an `approve`
+   sent to `0x833589fC...` **on mainnet**, where that address is not USDC. Meanwhile
+   `useScaffoldReadContract` reads the balance from Base regardless
+   (`useScaffoldReadContract.ts:35-46`), so the UI shows a correct Base balance while the writes
+   go somewhere else. The header does render a "Wrong network" dropdown
+   (`RainbowKitCustomConnectButton/index.tsx:41-43`), but it does not disable the page's buttons.
+2. **Pre-flight simulation** (`:118-124`, `simulateContractWriteAndNotifyError`). Without it, a
+   revert (insufficient allowance, insufficient balance) surfaces only as a wallet-level failure
+   or a failed on-chain tx that costs real gas.
+3. **`useTransactor` toasts** (`:138`) — "waiting for confirmation" / "transaction completed" /
+   block explorer link. There is currently zero feedback that a payout succeeded.
+4. **`isMining`**, which stays true until the receipt lands. Wagmi's `isPending` flips false as
+   soon as the wallet returns the hash — see issue 4.
+
+**Fix:** two hooks, and drop the `deployedContracts` import entirely:
+
+```ts
+const { writeContractAsync: writeUsdc, isMining: isApproving } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "USDC" });
+const { writeContractAsync: writePayouts, isMining: isPaying } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "Payouts" });
+
+await writeUsdc({ functionName: "approve", args: [payoutsAddress, parseUnits(amount, 6)] });
+await writePayouts({ functionName: "pay", args: [recipient, parseUnits(amount, 6), memo] });
+```
+
+Get `payoutsAddress` from `useDeployedContractInfo({ contractName: "Payouts" })` rather than
+indexing `deployedContracts[8453]` (see issue 6).
+
+### 3. Every error is swallowed into `console.error` — failures are silent to the user
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:44-46, 59-61`
+
+```ts
+} catch (e) {
+  console.error(e);
+}
+```
+
+Both handlers catch and log. A user who rejects in their wallet, is on the wrong chain, has
+insufficient allowance, or typed a malformed address sees **nothing at all** — the button
+re-enables and the form sits there. For an app that moves real money, "I clicked Send Payout and
+nothing happened" is the worst possible failure mode: the likely user response is to click again.
+
+`AGENTS.md` specifies the intended pattern, and the utilities are already in the repo
+(`packages/nextjs/utils/scaffold-eth/notification.tsx`, `getParsedError.ts`):
+
+```ts
+import { getParsedError, notification } from "~~/utils/scaffold-eth";
+
+} catch (e) {
+  notification.error(getParsedError(e));
+}
+```
+
+Note that switching to `useScaffoldWriteContract` (issue 2) already notifies on simulation
+failure and wrong network, so this `catch` becomes the backstop rather than the only handler.
+
+### 4. Approve → Pay has no allowance check and no wait for the approval to confirm
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:36-62`
+
+Three separate problems in the same flow:
+
+- **Nothing reads the current allowance.** The USDC ABI exposes `allowance`
+  (`contracts/deployedContracts.ts:74`) and it is never called. The user has no way to know
+  whether Approve is needed, and always pays for two transactions — including on a repeat payout
+  where allowance already covers the amount.
+- **`isPending` is not `isMining`.** Wagmi's `isPending` is true only while the wallet request is
+  outstanding; it goes false the moment the hash is returned, *before* the approval is mined. So
+  the moment the user confirms Approve in their wallet, `Send Payout` re-enables — click it and
+  `transferFrom` reverts against a zero allowance (`Payouts.sol:24`), burning gas.
+- **One shared `isPending` for two buttons** (`:101-110`): approving puts a spinner on *both*
+  buttons, so the UI cannot express "approval mined, now send."
+
+**Fix:** read the allowance, derive button state from it, and use two independent
+`isMining` flags:
+
+```ts
+const { data: allowance } = useScaffoldReadContract({
+  contractName: "USDC",
+  functionName: "allowance",
+  args: [connectedAddress, payoutsAddress],
+});
+
+const parsedAmount = amount ? parseUnits(amount, 6) : 0n;
+const needsApproval = parsedAmount > 0n && (allowance ?? 0n) < parsedAmount;
+```
+
+Then render only the button that applies (`needsApproval ? <Approve/> : <Send/>`), and disable
+`Send` while `isApproving`. Consider approving `parsedAmount` exactly (current behaviour, safer)
+but surface to the user that a second approval is needed if they raise the amount afterwards —
+today, editing the amount up after approving silently guarantees a revert.
+
+### 5. No input validation — empty or malformed input throws inside viem and is discarded
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:55, 78-113`
+
+```ts
+args: [recipient as `0x${string}`, parseUnits(amount || "0", 6), memo],
+```
+
+- `recipient` is a free-text `<input type="text">` with a bare `as \`0x${string}\`` cast. The cast
+  is a lie to the compiler; there is no `isAddress` check anywhere. An empty string or a typo
+  makes viem throw `InvalidAddressError`, which lands in the silent `catch` from issue 3.
+- `parseUnits(amount || "0", 6)` sends `0` when the field is blank. `Payouts.pay` reverts on
+  `amount == 0` (`Payouts.sol:23`), but only after the user has signed.
+- `<input type="number">` accepts negative values and `e` notation. `parseUnits("-5", 6)` yields a
+  negative bigint, which fails ABI encoding for `uint256` — again silently.
+- Neither button is ever `disabled` on empty input; both are clickable on a blank form.
+
+**Fix:** use the SE-2 inputs, which `AGENTS.md` requires and which solve validation and ENS
+resolution at once:
+
+```tsx
+import { AddressInput, IntegerInput } from "@scaffold-ui/components";
+
+<AddressInput value={recipient} onChange={setRecipient} placeholder="Recipient address or ENS" />
+```
+
+and gate the submit:
+
+```ts
+const isValid = isAddress(recipient) && parsedAmount > 0n && parsedAmount <= (usdcBalance ?? 0n);
+<button disabled={!isValid || isPaying} …>
+```
+
+Checking against `usdcBalance` also catches the over-spend case client-side instead of letting
+`transferFrom` revert.
+
+### 6. `USDC` was hand-added to the autogenerated `deployedContracts.ts` — the next deploy deletes it
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts:50-51` (and the file header, `:1-4`)
+
+```
+/**
+ * This file is autogenerated by Scaffold-ETH.
+ * You should not edit it manually or your changes might be overwritten.
+ */
+```
+
+The `USDC` entry is not produced by any deploy script — `packages/hardhat/deploy/00_deploy_payouts.ts`
+deploys only `Payouts` and passes the USDC address as a constructor arg. So `USDC` was pasted into
+a generated file. The next `yarn deploy` regenerates this file from the deployments directory and
+the `USDC` entry vanishes, at which point `useScaffoldReadContract({ contractName: "USDC" })` and
+the page's `deployedContracts[8453].USDC` both break — and `deployedContracts[8453].USDC` is a
+**build-time** reference (`page.tsx:11`), so this becomes a type error or a runtime
+`Cannot read properties of undefined`.
+
+`externalContracts.ts` exists for exactly this and is currently empty
+(`packages/nextjs/contracts/externalContracts.ts:14`).
+
+**Fix:** move the `USDC` block out of `deployedContracts.ts` and into `externalContracts.ts`:
+
+```ts
+const externalContracts = {
+  8453: {
+    USDC: { address: "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913", abi: [...] },
+  },
+} as const;
+```
+
+The scaffold hooks read from both files transparently, so no call site changes — and the direct
+`deployedContracts[8453]` indexing at `page.tsx:10-11` should go away anyway (see issue 2), since
+hardcoding chain `8453` breaks the moment anyone adds a testnet to `targetNetworks`.
+
+### 7. Production ships on Scaffold-ETH's shared demo Alchemy key and WalletConnect project ID
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:17, 27, 38`; `packages/nextjs/.env.example`
+
+```ts
+export const DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY = "IZYEU2cWBgnFmgiTAgpWD";
+alchemyApiKey: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY || DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY,
+walletConnectProjectId: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID || '3a8170812b534d0ff9d794f19a901d64',
+```
+
+Both fall back to the public SE-2 demo credentials shared by every scaffold project on the
+internet. Neither is set anywhere in the repo — `.env.example` has them blank and there is no
+`.env.local`. Combined with issue 1, the RPC fallback ordering in
+`services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx:20` also degrades: when the default key is detected, Alchemy is
+demoted *behind* the unkeyed public endpoint, so production traffic lands on Base's public RPC.
+
+The WalletConnect ID additionally drives what users see in their wallet: the connection prompt
+will read `scaffold-eth-2` (`services/web3/wagmiConnectors.tsx:49`), not your product name. For an
+app asking people to approve USDC spending, an unrecognized app name in the signing prompt is a
+trust problem, not just a cosmetic one.
+
+**Fix:** before launch, provision your own Alchemy app and WalletConnect project, set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` in the Vercel project
+env (Production + Preview), and set `appName` in `wagmiConnectors.tsx:49` to the real product
+name. Verify after deploy that `scaffoldConfig.alchemyApiKey !== DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` in the
+deployed bundle — the fallback fails silently, so a missing env var looks like a working app
+right up until you hit the rate limit.
+
+### 8. The landing page is still Scaffold-ETH boilerplate, with a link that is broken on Base
+
+**Files:** `packages/nextjs/app/page.tsx:20-72`; `packages/nextjs/components/Header.tsx:29-33`;
+`packages/nextjs/app/layout.tsx:10-13`
+
+`/` is the unmodified SE-2 template: *"Welcome to Scaffold-ETH 2"*, *"Get started by editing
+packages/nextjs/app/page.tsx"*, *"Edit your smart contract YourContract.sol"*. This is the first
+thing a real user sees.
+
+Worse, it links to `/blockexplorer` (`page.tsx:65`) — the built-in explorer only works against a
+local hardhat chain. The Footer correctly hides that link off local networks
+(`Footer.tsx:33-41`), but the home page links to it unconditionally, so on Base it leads to a page
+that cannot function.
+
+The header also exposes a **Debug Contracts** tab (`Header.tsx:29-33`) pointing at `/debug`, which
+gives any visitor a raw write interface to the deployed contracts.
+
+Page metadata is likewise still `'Scaffold-ETH 2 App'` / `'Built with 🏗 Scaffold-ETH 2'`
+(`layout.tsx:11-12`), which is what appears in the browser tab, in search results, and in every
+Slack/Twitter link preview via `getMetadata`'s OpenGraph tags.
+
+**Fix:**
+- Rewrite `app/page.tsx` as a real landing page, or redirect `/` to `/payouts`.
+- Remove the `/blockexplorer` card from `app/page.tsx:61-70`.
+- Gate the Debug tab on local networks, matching the Footer's pattern:
+  `...(targetNetwork.id === hardhat.id ? [{ label: "Debug Contracts", href: "/debug", … }] : [])`.
+- Set real `title` / `description` in `layout.tsx:10-13` and replace `public/thumbnail.jpg`.
