+## Should-fix
+
+### 9. `pollingInterval: 30000` is far too slow for Base
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:22`
+
+Base produces a block every ~2 seconds. `AGENTS.md` explicitly says to *decrease* the polling
+interval for L2s; this raises it to 30s. The value feeds `wagmiConfig`'s client
+(`services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx:20`) and therefore `useBlockNumber`, which is what drives balance
+refetching in `useScaffoldReadContract.ts:64-77` and the live-event poll in
+`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:115`. Net effect: after a payout confirms, the sender's USDC balance
+and the payout feed stay stale for up to half a minute.
+
+**Fix:** `pollingInterval: 5000`. Combined with issue 1's fix the RPC load is manageable; with
+issue 1 unfixed, lowering this makes things worse, so fix them together.
+
+### 10. Hardcoded `bg-black text-white` breaks the theme system
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:65, 121`
+
+```tsx
+<div className="min-h-screen bg-black text-white">
+…
+<div className="border border-gray-700 rounded p-3">
+```
+
+The app ships a daisyUI light/dark theme pair (`styles/globals.css:10-13`) and a working theme
+switcher in the footer (`Footer.tsx:43`). This page ignores both: it is pure black regardless of
+theme, so in light mode the header and footer are light and the page body is black. `AGENTS.md`
+also calls for daisyUI classes over raw Tailwind.
+
+The black background is also applied to a `min-h-screen` div nested inside the layout's
+`flex-1 main` (`ScaffoldEthAppWithProviders.tsx:22`), which forces the page taller than the
+viewport and leaves dead scroll space under the fold.
+
+**Fix:** drop `min-h-screen bg-black text-white` and let the layout theme apply; use
+`border-base-300` instead of `border-gray-700`, and wrap the form in a daisyUI
+`card bg-base-100 shadow-xl` to match the rest of the app.
+
+### 11. `btn loading` is a no-op in daisyUI 5 — the buttons show no spinner
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:101, 108`
+
+```tsx
+className={`btn btn-primary ${isPending ? "loading" : ""}`}
+```
+
+The repo is on daisyUI 5.5.19 (`package.json`, and the build log confirms `🌼 daisyUI 5.5.19`).
+daisyUI 5 removed `loading` as a button modifier; it is now a standalone element class. So the
+button greys out via `disabled` but gives no indication that anything is in flight — on Base,
+where confirmation takes a couple of seconds, that reads as an unresponsive button.
+
+**Fix:**
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPaying || !isValid}>
+  {isPaying && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-xs" />}
+  Send Payout
+</button>
+```
+
+### 12. Payout feed uses array index as React key
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:120-121`
+
+```tsx
+{(payouts ?? []).map((payout, i) => (
+  <div key={i} …>
+```
+
+With `watch: true`, new events are **prepended** (`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:247, 271`), so every
+new payout shifts every index. React then reconciles by position rather than identity, remounting
+the whole list and producing visible flicker; it also fights the historical-vs-live merge, which
+already dedupes on a composite key.
+
+**Fix:** use the same identity the hook dedupes on
+(`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:276`):
+`key={`${payout.transactionHash}-${payout.logIndex}`}`.
+
+### 13. Hand-rolled address truncation instead of the `Address` component
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:13, 122, 124`
+
+```ts
+const shorten = (addr?: string) => (addr ? `${addr.slice(0, 6)}...${addr.slice(-4)}` : "");
+```
+
+`AGENTS.md` requires `@scaffold-ui/components`' `Address`, which is already a dependency and used
+elsewhere (`app/page.tsx:27`). Reimplementing it loses ENS name resolution, ENS avatars, blockie
+avatars, copy-to-clipboard, and the block-explorer link. In a payouts feed, "who got paid" is the
+primary information and a bare `0x1234…abcd` is the least useful rendering of it.
+
+**Fix:** `<Address address={payout.args.to} chain={targetNetwork} />` and delete `shorten`.
+
+### 14. Feed is labelled ambiguously and renders attacker-controlled memo text
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:118, 127`
+
+The heading reads "Latest payouts" over an unfiltered global event query, so users see **every**
+payout anyone has ever made through the contract, not their own. Two consequences:
+
+- Users will reasonably read their own history into it and won't find their transactions.
+- `memo` is arbitrary sender-controlled text (`Payouts.sol:21`) displayed to every visitor. React
+  escapes it so there is no XSS, but nothing stops a spammer from emitting `pay()` calls of 1 wei
+  of USDC with memos like *"Claim your refund at evil.example"*. A public, unfiltered,
+  unmoderated text feed on a payments app is a phishing surface.
+
+Worth flagging to whoever owns product: on-chain memos are permanently public, so "What is this
+payout for?" invites users to publish information they may not intend to.
+
+**Fix:** filter to the connected user and relabel — `useScaffoldEventHistory` supports
+`filters: { from: connectedAddress }` (`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:89, 168`) — under a heading of
+"Your payouts". If a global feed is genuinely wanted, keep it as a separate, clearly labelled
+section and truncate/flag memos from unknown senders.
+
+### 15. `recipient` is not cleared after a successful payout
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:57-58`
+
+```ts
+setAmount("");
+setMemo("");
+```
+
+`amount` and `memo` reset; `recipient` persists. The form then reads as half-reset, with a
+populated recipient and blank amount — one of the easier ways to send a second payout to the
+wrong person. Either clear all three or clear none (and show a success state instead).
+
+**Fix:** add `setRecipient("")`, and pair it with a `notification.success` so the cleared form is
+unambiguously a success rather than a lost submission.
+
+### 16. CI is red: `--max-warnings=0` against 100 prettier warnings
+
+**File:** `.github/workflows/lint.yaml:30`
+
+```yaml
+- name: Run nextjs lint
+  run: yarn next:lint --max-warnings=0
+```
+
+`yarn next:lint` currently emits 100 `prettier/prettier` warnings (formatting drift in
+`scaffold.config.ts`, `next.config.ts`, `layout.tsx`, `getMetadata.ts`, `page.tsx`,
+`Header.tsx`, `wagmiConfig.tsx` — note `wagmiConfig.tsx:20` is a single ~700-character line).
+With `--max-warnings=0` this job fails on every push to `main`. A permanently-red pipeline in the
+week of launch means no one will notice a genuinely broken build.
+
+**Fix:** run `yarn format` at the repo root and commit. There is a Husky + lint-staged setup
+(`.husky/`, `.lintstagedrc.js`) that should have caught this, so it is worth checking hooks are
+actually installed locally for the team.
+
+### 17. CI never builds or type-checks
+
+**File:** `.github/workflows/lint.yaml`
+
+The only workflow runs lint. Nothing runs `next build` or `yarn check-types`, so a type error or
+prerender failure reaches Vercel unnoticed. This matters more than usual given issue 18.
+
+**Fix:** add to the same job:
+
+```yaml
+- name: Type check
+  run: yarn next:check-types
+- name: Build
+  run: yarn next:build
+```
+
+### 18. Documented deploy command disables TypeScript build errors
+
+**Files:** `packages/nextjs/next.config.ts:7-9`; `packages/nextjs/package.json` (`vercel:yolo`); `AGENTS.md`
+
+```ts
+typescript: { ignoreBuildErrors: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_IGNORE_BUILD_ERROR === "true" }
+```
+
+`vercel:yolo` sets `NEXT_PUBLIC_IGNORE_BUILD_ERROR=true`, and `AGENTS.md` names
+`yarn vercel:yolo --prod` as *the* frontend deploy command. That's a reasonable escape hatch for
+prototyping and the wrong default for a production payments app — it is exactly the mechanism that
+would let issue 6 (`deployedContracts[8453].USDC` becoming `undefined`) ship as a runtime crash
+instead of failing the build.
+
+**Fix:** use `yarn vercel --prod` for the production deploy. Type-checking currently passes
+(`tsc --noEmit` is clean), so nothing is lost by enforcing it.
+
+### 19. `vercel.json` pins no Node version or build command
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/vercel.json`
+
+```json
+{ "installCommand": "yarn install" }
+```
+
+Nothing pins the Node major version, so Vercel's default moves under you between deploys. Given
+this is a yarn 4 workspace with `nmHoistingLimits: workspaces`, and given Note 1 below, an
+unpinned runtime is avoidable risk the week of a launch.
+
+**Fix:** add `"engines": { "node": "22.x" }` to `packages/nextjs/package.json` (or set the Node
+version in Vercel project settings) so local, CI, and production agree.
